      "contentType": "story",
      "theme": "wonder",
      "title": "A smaller disturbance",
      "body": "Slapped the water and went under. Came up to see how much of the pond I'd moved. Only rings.\n\nTried again. Missed it again.\n\nThe third time I hardly lifted my tail. A little plop. I saw all of that.",
      "photoId": 72865483,
      "observationId": 45942438,
      "mediaSha1": "f532390e2064c16bb35a2932d5e7a57b5430c472",
      "localPath": "/tmp/freed-land138/72865483.jpg",
      "visibleScene": "A beaver at the surface of warm-lit pond water has its head/body low while a large bright splash erupts behind it. Fine droplets arc above the impact and concentric ripples cross the surface. The source explicitly describes a tail slap. Animal partly submerged; no other beaver or predator visible.",
      "fictionBoundary": "The desire to inspect her own splash, two repeats, missed views and gentle final plop are original fiction around the documented slap/dive. They are not a reported behavioral experiment or a claim beavers cannot ever slap without diving. The photographed large splash belongs to an early attempt, not the smaller final one. Source chronology among eight images is not inferred.",
      "canon": "Existing adult female Berta Bank, North American beaver Castor canadensis. Preserve southern Quebec wooded freshwater pond home (46.2,-74.6), avatar and all three admitted bodies. Source Manitoba is provenance, not relocation. Append among adult episodes without claiming season sequence. Distinct from grooming pleasure, ice-surface search and hesitant tree-felling: here force and her own viewing position prevent the experience she wants.",
      "payoff": "She can make an impressive disturbance or stay to see it. Reducing the force finally lets her witness the unimpressive version. The physical experiment ends in a small visible success without a lesson.",
      "reviewFlag": "Backlit splash is crisp and attractive; beaver body is partly submerged and smaller than a portrait. Lead must assess subject readability at feed size. Biological norm is alarm-related tail slapping, while her curiosity and repetition are explicitly fictional intentions."
    }
  ],
